  "provider_query": "The first YouTube video, Me at the zoo",
  "answer": "Here are relevant results on YouTube’s first video, **“Me at the zoo.”**\n\n1. **Original upload** — [Me at the zoo](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNQXAC9IVRw) (channel: jawed). About 19 seconds, uploaded April 23, 2005 (listed around April 24 UTC). Jawed Karim stands in front of elephants at the San Diego Zoo and says they have “really, really, really long trunks.” Hundreds of millions of views. [web:1]\n\n2. **What he says** — Transcript: “All right, so here we are, in front of the elephants… the cool thing about these guys is that they have really… really really long trunks and that's cool… and that's pretty much all there is to say.” [web:9]\n\n3. **Who uploaded it** — Jawed Karim, a YouTube co-founder with Chad Hurley and Steve Chen. The site was meant to make sharing video simple. [web:1][web:10]\n\n4. **San Diego Zoo’s own tribute** — [Us at the Zoo: A 20-year tribute](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Nq40445sn2M) (San Diego Zoo). Marks 20 years since the first YouTube video was filmed there. The zoo’s comment on the original (“We’re so honored…”) is among the most liked comments on the video. [web:4][web:9]\n\n5. **Local news recap** — [1st-ever YouTube video was taken at the San Diego Zoo](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MYw_nV9vM-0) (ABC 10 News). Notes the April 23, 2005 date, ~19 seconds, Karim (then about 25), and Google’s later purchase of YouTube. [web:2]\n\n6. **Background explainer** — [Me at the zoo | YouTube Geographic](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dcZY3yi8Xbw) (EmpLemon). Covers Karim, the low-res clip, and the story that YouTube grew partly from frustration at not finding video of the 2004 Super Bowl halftime incident online. [web:10]\n\n7. **Was it really first?** — [Was Me At The Zoo ACTUALLY The First Video On YouTube?](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OEzvINOXcqU) (Timeworks). Cites Karim saying he uploaded it to a test database that became the live one, so it was the first video. [web:5]\n\n8. **Claims it wasn’t first (widely disputed)** — Videos such as [“Me at the zoo” Wasn’t the First YouTube Video…](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5EurHP1DCJg) argue an earlier test clip existed; debunks point to date glitches and to uploads only being possible from April 23, 2005. [web:3][web:8]\n\n**Short version:** “Me at the zoo” is the earliest public YouTube video still on the site: a brief elephant clip by co-founder Jawed Karim at the San Diego Zoo on April 23, 2005.",
